{#-
  THE FORWARD NOTE on a superseded page.

  Policy, 12 August 2026: a page that has been reviewed, approved and linked to
  from outside is LOCKED. After that, changes become new versions rather than
  edits, and the old page stays live carrying a note to the new one — so nothing
  breaks and the evolution stays visible.

  This partial renders that note. It is deliberately at the TOP of the article
  and outside `data-pagefind-body`'s useful content: a reader who arrives from an
  old citation needs to know before they read, not after.

  WHY THE OLD PAGE IS NOT REDIRECTED. A redirect would serve the newest text to
  someone who cited the old one, silently, which is the failure
  src/chapter-aliases.njk exists to prevent — "it silently starts pointing at
  something else, which is strictly worse, because nothing signals the breakage
  to either side." The whole point of a version is that the cited text stays
  where it was cited.

  NO `noindex` HERE, deliberately, and this is the opposite call from the
  redirect stubs. A superseded version is not a duplicate of the current one —
  it is a different document that says different things, and it is meant to be
  findable. It carries a canonical pointing at ITSELF (base.njk's default),
  because it is the canonical copy of what it says.
-#}
{% if superseded_by %}
<aside class="superseded-note" role="note">
  <p class="superseded-note__lede">
    <strong>This version is kept as filed.</strong>
    {% if superseded_on %}It was superseded on
    <time datetime="{{ superseded_on }}">{{ superseded_on }}</time>.{% endif %}
  </p>
  <p>
    It has not been edited since, and it is not the current text.
    <a href="/star-rangers{{ superseded_by }}">Read the version that supersedes it</a>.
  </p>
</aside>
{% endif %}
{% if version_of %}
<aside class="supersedes-note" role="note" data-pagefind-ignore>
  <p>
    This is a revised version. The
    <a href="/star-rangers{{ version_of }}">earlier version</a>
    is kept as it was filed.
  </p>
</aside>
{% endif %}
